<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf III, Count of Holstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1160–1225</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Adolf III of Schauenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf III (1160–1225) was count of Holstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a> from the house of Schauenburg. Around 1188 he granted merchants land to found a new town beside the old cathedral settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, the origin of the city's commercial growth, and obtained privileges for its trade. He took part in the Third Crusade and lost Holstein to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1201.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-iii-of-holstein.jpg" alt="Adolf III, Count of Holstein" width="320" height="670" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlotte Heineccius · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf_III._Graf_von_Schauenburg_-_Engelbert_Peiffer_-_Trostbr%C3%BCcke_Hamburg2_close.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
